<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qal'at Beni Hammad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1007</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Kalâa des Béni Hammad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qal'at Beni Hammad is the ruined first capital of the Hammadid dynasty, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hodna-mountains">Hodna Mountains</a> of north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, founded around 1007 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammad-ibn-buluggin">Hammad ibn Buluggin</a>. It grew into a city of palaces, mosques and workshops, whose great minaret still stands, and was famed for its glazed ceramics. Abandoned for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Béjaïa</a> from the late 11th century, it was sacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> in 1152 and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1980.</p></div>
